17 #edb/ref 17592186310060 13194139534785 true] [17592186310057 1089 "q-326267" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310057 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534785 true] [17592186310057 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/3497daefa76dd730942b7deb1f32c42a30823c9de953f45ec011218aff126b21.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $9,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $J(2,0).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310057 1113 #uuid "e757c132-c765-59f3-be49-296fcd0ef862" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310057 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=9.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310058 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310059 13194139534785 true] [17592186310058 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534785 true] [17592186310058 1041 "field-1" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310058 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310059 13194139534785 true] [17592186310058 1110 #uuid "6836ee91-2196-5abe-8d87-442849908878" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310059 1034 "2\\cos(\\theta)" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310059 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534785 true] [17592186310059 1111 #uuid "3233f5de-29c3-5b81-a289-815356f700f9" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310060 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310061 13194139534785 true] [17592186310060 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534785 true] [17592186310060 1041 "field-2" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310060 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310061 13194139534785 true] [17592186310060 1110 #uuid "9160bfd8-08a2-59b0-ac63-202c2c2e9e56" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310061 1034 "9\\sin(\\theta)" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310061 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534785 true] [17592186310061 1111 #uuid "65685c40-555c-5428-b23d-3a3844a0da86" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310062 1034 "3\\sin(\\theta)" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310062 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534785 true] [17592186310062 1111 #uuid "ba6b88a5-43fb-5246-b8fd-808ca702cf9d" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310063 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310064 13194139534785 true] [17592186310063 1089 "q-327803" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310063 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534785 true] [17592186310063 1100 "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310063 1113 #uuid "3c978aef-b5f2-571e-91d6-0c640846f534" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310063 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter  $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{10} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{10}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12 \\\\ \\frac{y-12}{3\\sqrt{11}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{11})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99}=1.\n$$" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310065 13194139534785 true] [17592186310064 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534785 true] [17592186310064 1041 "field-1" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310064 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310065 13194139534785 true] [17592186310064 1110 #uuid "fe81c360-ef75-5866-b642-fff70e9b7d28" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310065 1034 "\\frac{(x+4)^2}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^2}{99}" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310065 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534785 true] [17592186310065 1111 #uuid "37a96b6f-90f6-5067-a4e1-61d8a9d34747" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310066 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310067 13194139534785 true] [17592186310066 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310069 13194139534785 true] [17592186310066 1089 "q-327822" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310066 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534785 true] [17592186310066 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/31b10310b585d867ecf883db2a5d778ec5ec9160df03fc4487d2ef7fd0b9a3af.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310066 1113 #uuid "70bf7d5a-49a5-578b-bafa-224f26540a89" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310066 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/73cbae2c39fb2466fd59ced196c08ff2b6131b08a6a5dd4933e736404b12266e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310067 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310068 13194139534785 true] [17592186310067 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534785 true] [17592186310067 1041 "field-1" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310067 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310068 13194139534785 true] [17592186310067 1110 #uuid "7227bee1-b461-5fd2-9c93-bbe1d1ef17de" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310068 1034 "0" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310068 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534785 true] [17592186310068 1111 #uuid "19ad4ec0-c881-5ae7-a382-6cc3818c0abe" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310069 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310070 13194139534785 true] [17592186310069 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534785 true] [17592186310069 1041 "field-2" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310069 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310070 13194139534785 true] [17592186310069 1110 #uuid "50cd9554-87d7-53e9-8226-f536e8c03638" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310070 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310070 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534785 true] [17592186310070 1111 #uuid "7200d9ef-eca3-5310-a6aa-8dcdc8e46cf8" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310071 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310072 13194139534785 true] [17592186310071 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310074 13194139534785 true] [17592186310071 1089 "q-328327" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310071 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534785 true] [17592186310071 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/5234672d8176a0a04bbc3feecfe13092ff116a25682ae055286f1fe858b8a754.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310071 1113 #uuid "b60b26b2-8291-58d7-a30a-dee3279193ee" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310071 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-1,2),$ $a=5,$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the horizontal line through its center.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/1b2d7617af37197dfd2702b4497263e563458b0bf11c622308220def93b688a9.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}],$ we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310072 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310073 13194139534785 true] [17592186310072 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534785 true] [17592186310072 1041 "field-1" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310072 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310073 13194139534785 true] [17592186310072 1110 #uuid "6bd7e3df-4fde-5785-b280-a77314580cef" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310073 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534785 true] [17592186310073 1111 #uuid "8fdaf5a5-fce3-56ec-af9d-3d07ff93b92f" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310074 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310075 13194139534785 true] [17592186310074 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534785 true] [17592186310074 1041 "field-2" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310074 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310075 13194139534785 true] [17592186310074 1110 #uuid "4b647bdb-2df2-500e-8d81-f14155ea2163" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310075 1034 "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534785 true] [17592186310075 1111 #uuid "a93c1fdc-81e0-548e-8bb7-d374cb5c868e" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310076 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310077 13194139534785 true] [17592186310076 1089 "q-79290" 13194139534785 true] [17592186310076 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534785 true] [17592186310076 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/e5d32d7b081238d3717a03f7718fcf8e3de8a7a00e5a04eada0129d1bcc43dc3.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-2,5)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterc